== ψ Implementation ==

=== The Bifilar Coil as Optimal ψ Transducer ===

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = Why the bifilar geometry is the correct ψ transducer
| step1_label = Goal: maximise F²μν
| step1_text  = The coupling source term is <math>S = \alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} = \alpha \cdot 2(B^2 - E^2)</math>. To maximise <math>|S|</math>, maximise <math>|B^2 - E^2|</math>. If we can make <math>B \approx 0</math> while maintaining large <math>E</math>, then <math>|S| = 2\alpha E^2</math> — maximally efficient.
| step2_label = Bifilar cancels B
| step2_text  = Two parallel traces carrying equal and opposite currents (<math>+I</math> and <math>-I</math>) produce magnetic fields that cancel in the far field: <math>B_{\rm net} \approx 0</math> for observation points at distances large compared to the trace separation <math>d_{\rm gap}</math>. This is the defining property of bifilar (twin-lead) geometry.
| step3_label = Bifilar concentrates E
| step3_text  = The same opposite voltages on adjacent traces create a strong E-field between them: <math>E_{\rm bif} = V/(d_{\rm gap})</math>. This E-field is concentrated in the gap volume — exactly where the BST composite fill sits, maximising the F²μν integral over the composite volume.
| step4_label = ICNIRP compliance
| step4_text  = Because B ≈ 0 in the far field (> 1 cm from traces), the bifilar geometry is the only way to achieve high E² at the composite (for transduction) while maintaining ICNIRP-compliant far-field B exposure for the operator. A single-trace coil would require impractically low current to stay within ICNIRP B-field limits.
| step5_label = Self-detecting property
| step5_text  = In reverse transduction (ψ → EM): a changing ψ field in the composite perturbs ε_eff(t), which modulates the capacitance between the bifilar traces, producing a measurable back-EMF. The same bifilar coil that drives the transducer also acts as its own detector. No separate pick-up coil is needed for basic ψ detection.
| step6_label = Mode shape matching
| step6_text  = The bifilar trace pattern on the TPMS shell surface is not uniform — it follows the amplitude contours of the target ψ₀₁₀ mode. Traces are denser where the mode amplitude is high (equatorial region) and sparser at the poles. This pattern optimises the overlap integral <math>\int E^2_{\rm bif}\psi_{010}\,d^3x</math>.
}}

=== Bifilar Trace Optimisation ===

The overlap integral is maximised when the bifilar E-field distribution is spatially proportional to the target mode shape:

:<math>E^2_{\rm bif}(\mathbf{x}) \propto |\psi_{010}(\mathbf{x})|^2</math>

This is achieved by varying the local trace density (turns per unit area) to be proportional to <math>|\psi_{010}|</math>. The trace pattern is computed from:

# Run COMSOL eigenvalue solve to obtain <math>\psi_{010}(\mathbf{x})</math> on the shell surface.
# Map the mode amplitude to local winding density: <math>\rho_{\rm turns}(\mathbf{x}) \propto |\psi_{010}(\mathbf{x})|</math>.
# Generate the bifilar trace routing from this density map using a space-filling curve algorithm (Hilbert curve or spiral path following amplitude contours).
# Verify by computing the overlap integral numerically; target <math>\eta_{\rm overlap} > 0.85</math>.

=== BST Bias Dependence of Conversion Efficiency ===

From [[ψ Permittivity]] (B2), the BST composite permittivity varies with bias voltage: <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(V)</math>. This modifies the effective coupling:

:<math>S_{\rm device} = \alpha\,\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(V)\,E^2_{\rm bifilar}</math>

Higher bias (lower <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}</math>) → lower source term → lower conversion efficiency, but also shifts the impedance <math>Z_\psi = (1/\omega^2)\sqrt{\mu_\psi/\varepsilon_\psi}</math> to better match the operator's biological impedance (from [[ψ Impedance Theory]] A4). The [[Anchor Controller]] (D6) optimises the bias voltage for the combined objective of maximum power transfer (impedance match) rather than maximum raw conversion efficiency.

