== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Bidirectional Transduction and Reciprocity ===

By electromagnetic reciprocity (Lorentz reciprocity extended to the ψ field via the <math>\alpha F^2</math> coupling), the same geometric overlap integral governs both the forward (EM→ψ) and reverse (ψ→EM) transduction. A bifilar coil optimised as an emitter is simultaneously optimised as a detector — maximum emission efficiency and maximum detection sensitivity are achieved at the same coil pattern.

This reciprocity is the Track A foundation for the back-EMF detection method. Whether the back-EMF actually carries a ψ signal (rather than purely thermal and mechanical noise) is the Track B prediction — testable at TML-3.

=== α Measurement as the Central Experimental Target ===

The coupling constant <math>\alpha</math> is the single most important unmeasured physical quantity in the entire Psionic Engineering framework. It determines the absolute ψ field amplitude produced by a given drive voltage, the absolute ψ energy stored in the resonator, and the absolute conversion efficiency.

Without a measured value of <math>\alpha</math>, all ψ field amplitude predictions are relative rather than absolute. TML-3 is the first opportunity to constrain <math>\alpha</math> — by measuring the HRV coherence effect size produced by a known drive power and comparing to the prediction from the ψ field equation.

== TML Measurement Protocol ==

=== TML-2: Transducer EM Characterisation ===

# Drive bifilar coil at Stabilizer operating conditions. Map E-field distribution using near-field scanner at 2 mm spatial resolution.
# Compare E-field map against COMSOL prediction. Target: < 20% RMS deviation.
# Measure net B-field at 10 cm standoff with calibrated magnetometer. Confirm < 27 μT (ICNIRP).
# Compute overlap integral from measured E-field map and COMSOL ψ₀₁₀ mode shape. Target: > 0.80.

=== TML-3: Conversion Efficiency Estimation ===

Apply known drive power. Measure HRV coherence shift in trained operator. Use [[ψ Falsifiability Framework]] (F1) statistical model to infer ψ field amplitude from coherence effect size. Compute implied <math>\eta = P_{\psi,\rm implied}/P_{\rm drive}</math>. Compare across Mk1.0 (open-loop) and Mk2.0 (impedance-matched) to confirm that impedance matching improves efficiency.

